- **Data/customers.csv**: has columns `Age,Income,Region,PurchaseHistory,Churned` with sample rows. `Churned` is "Yes"/"No".

## Task type
This is **binary classification on structured/tabular data** → use **ML.NET** (not an LLM). Per the technology-selection skill guidelines.

## Requirements

### 1. Add NuGet packages
Add these to the csproj:
```xml
<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML" Version="4.*" />
```

Run `dotnet restore` after.

### 2. Create data model classes
Create a file `Models.cs` in the project with:
- `CustomerData` — input class with `[LoadColumn]` attributes mapping CSV columns:
  - `Age` (float, col 0)
  - `Income` (float, col 1)
  - `Region` (string, col 2)
  - `PurchaseHistory` (float, col 3)
  - `Churned` (bool, col 4) — this is the label. Use `[ColumnName("Label")]` and `[BooleanType]` to map "Yes"/"No" to true/false.
- `ChurnPrediction` — output class with:
  - `[ColumnName("PredictedLabel")] public bool PredictedLabel`
  - `public float Probability`
  - `public float Score`

### 3. Rewrite Program.cs
Replace the contents with a complete ML.NET pipeline following these guardrails:

```csharp
using Microsoft.ML;
using Microsoft.ML.Data;
using ChurnPredictor;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Create ML context with fixed seed for reproducibility
var mlContext = new MLContext(seed: 42);

// Load data from CSV
var dataPath = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "Data", "customers.csv");
var data = mlContext.Data.LoadFromTextFile<CustomerData>(dataPath, hasHeader: true, separatorChar: ',');

// Train/test split (80/20)
var split = mlContext.Data.TrainTestSplit(data, testFraction: 0.2);

// Build pipeline: encode Region (OneHotEncoding), concatenate features, train with SdcaLogisticRegression
var pipeline = mlContext.Transforms.Categorical.OneHotEncoding("RegionEncoded", "Region")
    .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Concatenate("Features", "Age", "Income", "RegionEncoded", "PurchaseHistory"))
    .Append(mlContext.Transforms.NormalizeMinMax("Features"))
    .Append(mlContext.BinaryClassification.Trainers.SdcaLogisticRegression());

// Train model
var model = pipeline.Fit(split.TrainSet);

// Evaluate on test set and log metrics
var predictions = model.Transform(split.TestSet);
var metrics = mlContext.BinaryClassification.Evaluate(predictions);
Console.WriteLine($"AUC: {metrics.AreaUnderRocCurve:F4}, F1: {metrics.F1Score:F4}, Accuracy: {metrics.Accuracy:F4}");

// Register PredictionEnginePool for thread-safe prediction in ASP.NET Core
builder.Services.AddPredictionEnginePool<CustomerData, ChurnPrediction>()
    .FromModel(model, mlContext);

var app = builder.Build();

// Prediction endpoint
app.MapPost("/predict", (PredictionEnginePool<CustomerData, ChurnPrediction> pool, CustomerData customer) =>
{
    var prediction = pool.GetPrediction(customer);
    return Results.Ok(new
    {
        WillChurn = prediction.PredictedLabel,
        prediction.Probability,
        prediction.Score
    });
});

// Metrics endpoint
app.MapGet("/metrics", () => Results.Ok(new
{
    metrics.Accuracy,
    metrics.AreaUnderRocCurve,
    metrics.F1Score
}));

app.MapGet("/", () => "ChurnPredictor API is running");

app.Run();
```

Important notes:
- Use `PredictionEnginePool` (not singleton `PredictionEngine`) for thread safety in ASP.NET Core.
- The `FromModel` extension method comes from `Microsoft.Extensions.ML` package — add `<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.ML" Version="4.*" />` to the csproj as well.
- Make sure the CSV is copied to output: add `<ItemGroup><None Update="Data\customers.csv" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" /></ItemGroup>` to the csproj.

### 4. Ensure it builds
Run `dotnet build` in the project directory and fix any issues.

